--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Node-RED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

HotLoop Flow is not affiliated with or endorsed by the Node-RED project or the
OpenJS Foundation.

HotLoop Flow reads and writes the Node-RED v1 flow file format, and implements
compatible semantics for property expressions, message routing, and the core node
set. That interoperability is deliberate: it is what lets an existing flow be
imported without being rewritten.

Most of it was written from the format and the behaviour, with its own structure.
Where an imported flow has to get exactly the answer Node-RED gives, the upstream
code was ported instead, and the files below are derivative works of Node-RED:

    Node-RED - https://github.com/node-red/node-red
    Copyright OpenJS Foundation and other contributors, https://openjsf.org/
    Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0 (LICENSE in this repository)

The upstream source files carry the line "Copyright JS Foundation and other
contributors, http://js.foundation", and the files ported from them carry it too.
Node-RED ships no NOTICE file, so there is none to pass on. All of these were
modified. Versions are the ones each port was checked against.

    internal/nodes/split.go
        @node-red/nodes 5.0.7, core/sequence/17-split.js: SplitNode
    internal/nodes/join.go
        @node-red/nodes 5.0.7, core/sequence/17-split.js: JoinNode and its
        reduce functions
    internal/nodes/batch.go
        @node-red/nodes 5.0.7, core/sequence/19-batch.js
    internal/nodes/linkcall.go
        @node-red/nodes 5.0.7, core/common/60-link.js: LinkCallNode, and
        LinkOutNode's return mode
    internal/nodes/common.go, LinkRegistry.target only
        @node-red/runtime 5.0.7, lib/nodes/index.js: linkcallTargets.getTargetNode
    internal/nodes/tlsconfig.go, newTLSConfig only
        @node-red/nodes 5.0.7, core/network/05-tls.js: the TLSConfig constructor
        and addTLSOptions
    internal/nodes/httpcookies.go
        @node-red/nodes 5.0.7, core/network/21-httpin.js: HTTPOut's msg.cookies;
        core/network/21-httprequest.js: msg.cookies into the cookie jar, and
        extractCookies
    internal/nodes/httpmultipart.go
        @node-red/nodes 5.0.7, core/network/21-httprequest.js: the
        multipart/form-data body
    internal/nodes/network_mqtt.go, the knownMediaTypes table only
        @node-red/nodes 5.0.7, core/network/10-mqtt.js
    internal/jsonata/jsonata.go, legacyRef only
        @node-red/util 5.0.7, lib/util.js: prepareJSONataExpression
    internal/engine/property.go, ParsePath only
        @node-red/util 5.0.7, lib/util.js: normalisePropertyExpression
    internal/nodes/testdata/http-golden/golden.mjs, the blocks marked verbatim
        @node-red/nodes 4.1.15 and 5.0.7 (identical in both),
        core/network/21-httpin.js and core/network/21-httprequest.js

Where HotLoop Flow deliberately diverges from Node-RED behaviour, the divergence is
documented at the point of divergence in the source and summarised in
docs/compatibility.md. Message cloning on fan-out, back-pressure on node inboxes,
and credential encryption are the three that change observable behaviour.

"Node-RED" is a trademark of the OpenJS Foundation. It is used here only to
describe compatibility.

